{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "MosaicAnthroEmissions",
    "description": "WRF-Chem's anthropogenic aerosol emissions into MOSAIC's 8 size sections, the anthropogenic part of `mosaic_addemiss` in chem/module_mosaic_addemiss.F (WRF v4.8.0), which emissions_driver calls for chem_opt = 170 (CBMZ_MOSAIC_KPP) whenever an emission input is read. It is the aerosol half of the `emiss_opt = 4` (ecbmz_mosaic) package; the gas half is CBMZAnthroEmissions. Sea-salt, dust and fire emissions, which the same routine also adds under their own switches (seas_opt, dust_opt, biomass_burn_opt), are not part of this component and are off in the reference configuration.\n\nThe scheme. The input carries surface mass fluxes (ug m-2 s-1, WRF's \"ug/m3 m/s\") of sulfate, nitrate, organic carbon, black carbon and unspeciated PM (MOSAIC's other-inorganic, OIN), split into size modes in one of two ways depending on the input variety `emiss_inpt_opt`. A RADM2-variety input (1 or 102) gives MADE/SORGAM Aitken (`i`) and accumulation (`j`) modes, plus a coarse PM10 field, and each mode is apportioned to the sections by the SORGAM fractions; a CBM-Z-variety input (101, PNNL_CM) gives fine (`j`) and coarse (`c`) fields apportioned by the MOSAIC fractions (MosaicEmissionParameters). Any other variety leaves every fraction zero, as WRF's `CASE DEFAULT: return` does (the TNO variety, 121, has fractions of its own that this component does not carry). The section mass flux of component x is therefore fr_f*x_j + fr_c*x_c + fr_i*x_i + fr_j*x_j, with the fractions of the unused variety zero, and OIN adds the SORGAM coarse fraction of PM10. Each section also gains particles: the emitted volume, the sum of each component's mass over its density, divided by the section's centre volume `volumcen` (MOSAIC's `volumcen_sect`, (pi/6) dcen^3, supplied by the consumer with the rest of the section geometry). Under emiss_opt = 4 the PM10 term is always zero, because that package has no E_PM_10 field and WRF reads the empty dummy slot in its place (FORTRAN_BUGS.md N116), so neither variety emits coarse OIN. Every flux becomes a tendency of the emission layer through `aerosol_layer_tendency`, mosaic_addemiss's `fact = (dtstep/dz8w)*alt`, and the increment WRF applies over a step is exactly dt times it.\n\nNH4, Na, Cl, Ca, CO3 and MSA are emitted only for the MOZART-MOSAIC mechanisms; for chem_opt 170 they are zero and carry no tendency here. The emission layer is one layer; a consumer with kemit > 1 maps the component over them.\n\nWhat the tests pin. Steps of the chem170 emissions capture (data/eqwefic/chem170_scm/run_emis.sh), once per input variety, asserting every section's tendency of every component and of the number against the real64 replay of the dumped inputs through chem/module_mosaic_addemiss.F (kernels/emissions_driver), at 1e-9 relative.",
    "authors": [
      "EqWeFiC"
    ],
    "created": "2026-11-12T00:00:00Z",
    "tags": [
      "wrf-chem",
      "emissions",
      "anthropogenic",
      "mosaic",
      "aerosol",
      "chem_opt-170"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "WRF-Chem v4.8.0, chem/module_mosaic_addemiss.F (subroutine mosaic_addemiss), called from chem/emissions_driver.F for the MOSAIC chem_opt values."
      },
      {
        "citation": "Zaveri, R. A., Easter, R. C., Fast, J. D. and Peters, L. K. (2008). Model for Simulating Aerosol Interactions and Chemistry (MOSAIC). J. Geophys. Res., 113, D13204."
      }
    ]
  },
  "index_sets": {
    "bin": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": 8
    }
  },
  "models": {
